<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">4816301</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2139566</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Brief Definitive Reports</subject><subj-group><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>THE K-CHAINS OF THE IMMUNOGLOBULIN FROM A CONTINUOUS CULTURE OF HUMAN LYMPHOCYTES (DAUDI) HAVE AN UNUSUAL MOLECULAR SIZE</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Kennel</surname><given-names>Stephen J.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x3505820N0x3868630">From the Department of Experimental Pathology, Scripps Clinic and Research Foundation, La Jolla, California 92037</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>1</day><month>4</month><year>1974</year></pub-date><volume>139</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>1031</fpage><lpage>1036</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>16</day><month>12</month><year>1973</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1974 by The Rockefeller University Press</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> Membrane-associated immunoglobulin (M-Ig) was isolated from Daudi cells which had been radioiodinated using lactoperoxidase. This M-Ig was found to have a molecular size on SDS-PAGE of 330,000 daltons. The component protein chains (&#x000b5; and K) have molecular sizes of 75,000 and 33,000 daltons, respectively. Further studies showed that [<sup>3</sup>H]galactose can be incorporated into K-chains, but that extra protein and not carbohydrate was responsible for the increased molecular size of this molecule. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>